module gunbc.interpreter_replacement_cut

import std.types { String, NonEmptyStr, List }
import gunbc.replacement_cut {
  ReplacementCutId,
  CoverageOpen,
  ConsumerDisposition, RetainSameContract, ReplacedByY, Retire, Undecided,
  HigherBoundaryAnswer, DescentParentUnauthored,
  EvidenceDisposition, ReenrollAgainstY, RetireEvidence,
  ExternalXAnswer, ExternalXRoles, ExternalDifferentialOracle, HistoricalMeasurementSource,
  LegacyConsumerSurveyRow, LegacyReplacementSurvey,
  CutRegistration, RegisteredCutRemeasureRequired,
}

data interpreter_replacement_cut_note: String = "RECAST 2026-08-28 FROM AN EXACT PLAN INTO A LEGACY SURVEY PLUS A REMEASUREMENT OBLIGATION, and everything below this paragraph is the historical record of the plan as it stood. WHAT CHANGED AND WHY: gunbc.replacement_cut re-grounded BoundaryConsumer onto the relation consumer x consumed-authority at DECLARATION grain. This value was authored at MODULE-OR-BINARY grain over a ten-of-125 sample -- it says so itself, several times, and those statements are the reason the recast is mechanical rather than a judgment call. The two are different identity universes, so the old value no longer inhabits the exact carrier. THAT IS THE DESIRED RESULT: the survey was not merely incomplete in count, it was authored in a universe too coarse to satisfy the new contract, and weakening the grain so it would fit is the one move that would launder a sample into an exactness claim. WHAT THE RE-GROUNDING FOUND, mechanically, which no reader of the strings had noticed: four of the ten consumer labels -- v1_compiler.cli_run, v1_compiler.coproduct_reflection, v1_compiler.pre_push, v1_compiler.v1_interpreter_dispatch_generated -- name no .dag module anywhere in the corpus. They are host Rust compilation units. A NonEmptyStr accepted them and a resolvable DeclarationRef does not, which is the argument for the re-grounding stated as a receipt rather than as a prediction. They are NOT residue and NOT unidentifiable: a Rust dependency on X is a real semantic consumer whose removal makes the build refuse, and gunbc.rust_item_identity plus gunbc.rust_item_host_observation item_declaration_ref already project a citable Rust item into the SAME DeclarationRef vocabulary, with typed Uncitable and Refused arms for impl methods and unprojectable paths. So the exact identities exist; what this row never measured is which ITEMS consume X, and one label may expand into many item relations, into an uncitable impl method, or into none at all. A one-for-one translation would have invented ten identities the measurement never established. THE OBLIGATION IS REGISTERED RATHER THAN DISCHARGED: interpreter_cut_registration is RegisteredCutRemeasureRequired, so the identity stays visible -- a descent naming it resolves and refuses as RegistryParentRemeasureRequired rather than as ParentAbsent -- while the coarse survey is structurally unable to answer as an exact plan. Remeasurement belongs to the lane that owns this boundary. ONE SUBSTANTIVE FINDING FOR THAT REMEASUREMENT, visible only at exact grain: v1_interpreter_dispatch_generated appears BOTH as a retired consumer and as a covered-region member, and an X-region member is not automatically an inbound boundary consumer -- so it is very likely a region member that never represented a boundary crossing.\n\nHISTORICAL RECORD OF THE PLAN FOLLOWS, unchanged except where it is quoted above. THE FIRST PRODUCTION ReplacementCut IN THE REPOSITORY. X is the v1 tree-walking interpreter; Y is the INDEPENDENTLY EMITTED v2 evaluator (v2.compiler.eval), not per-subject native emission -- see the Y1/Y2 correction below, which is the most consequential amendment this row has taken. The row is authored from a measured deletion rather than from imagination: the deletion was performed on integration/interpreter-cut and the boundary population read from the far side of it, which is the only side it is visible from.\n\nWHY THIS PLAN REFUSES, and why the refusal is the deliverable: admit_replacement_cut_plan returns PlanRefused with counted causes, and every cause is a TRUE open decision rather than an authoring defect. Two classes. UNDECIDED: the driver region is another lane's boundary and the cut above it is unauthored, so no disposition can be earned here. UNCOVERED SURVIVING: the consumers that must keep working have no coverage authority to name, because Y has no executing realization. CORRECTED (this cause was previously justified from the per-subject native routing roster -- 77 ReadsLiveTree plus 35 host-effect entries ceilinged into a lane that does not exist -- which is Y2 evidence offered for a Y1 contract, the exact conflation the Y1/Y2 paragraph below unpicks). The correct refusal is narrower, nearer, and measured: v2.compiler.eval has not earned a v2-emitter-produced executing receipt. Its frontier row is seed-retained with a RealizationGap located at ProbeStageAssemble, and the compiler-wide emitter_produced_baseline is 0 -- no module in the v2 compiler has a producer-qualified promotion. Naming a coverage authority anyway would be the fallback-wearing-a-disposition move the carrier already refuses; naming Y as covering a consumer Y does not cover is the same lie one level up. So the plan is authored to refuse and the causes are counted.\n\nWHAT THE COUNT IS, STATED AT ITS REAL SCOPE (corrected after review): the causes are the known unresolved dispositions AMONG THE SAMPLED CONSUMERS, UNDER ONE CANDIDATE Y. They are not a complete consumer census, not a semantic-feature census, and not a work estimate. An earlier revision of this note called them the honest size of what remains, which is an overclaim on two axes at once: the census enumerates ten of 125 files by its own declaration, and the refusal is evaluated against native execution alone.\n\nAND WHAT THE REFUSAL DOES NOT ESTABLISH (written when new_root still named native execution through emitted Rust, and kept as the record of that correction rather than rewritten as though it had always read this way): this plan's refusal showed that ONE realization does not presently cover the retained consumers. It does not show that no realization could. Any other candidate -- a fresh, smaller, fail-closed evaluator among them -- is excluded here BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by evidence, and no such candidate has been built, run, or refused. The choice of native execution is a received direction, not a conclusion derived in this carrier; a row that states a received constraint without saying so is indistinguishable from a row that concluded it. CORRECTED: new_root now names the boundary in CONSUMER terms and no longer in one realization's vocabulary. The two are different decisions and only the second is fixed by direction. Section 3 is explicit that the interface shape is what is owned, that a transport is one of N and never a fact about the dependency, and that the dispatch selecting a realization is itself realization and sits peripheral rather than in the interface. A replacement contract naming emitted Rust fused those, which is the mechanical reason every reading of this plan terminated at the native routing roster: that measures ONE REALIZATION'S COVERAGE and it is not the contract's denominator. The contract's denominator is reachable expression forms, reachable primitive identities, required effect kinds and required result shapes.\n\nTHE Y1/Y2 CORRECTION, and it supersedes the selected-realization paragraph this replaces. TWO DIFFERENT REPLACEMENTS were being run as one, and separating them dissolves most of what this row treated as blocking.\n\n  Y1 -- the emitted v2 evaluator. v2.compiler.eval, authored in .dag and emitted into an independently executable artifact. Another GENERIC evaluator: it executes arbitrary .dag declarations, exactly as X does.\n  Y2 -- direct per-subject native emission. Each .dag declaration compiled into its own Rust implementation, with no generic evaluator involved for that subject.\n\nThese are not the same migration, and the compiler model already carries both: v2.compiler.compile's CompileMode is TranslateTo -- target -- OR Eval -- runtime -- two sibling realizations, so generic evaluation is a modeled arm of the compiler and not a hypothetical.\n\nWHICH ONE DELETES X: Y1. An emitted v2 evaluator could replace the v1 interpreter WHILE EVERY SUBJECT REMAINS DYNAMICALLY EVALUATED. Y2 is a separate, later replacement whose X is generic evaluation itself and which may eventually retire Y1 too -- it is not a prerequisite for deleting v1. Treating it as one is what produced this row's central defect: a Y1-shaped CONTRACT -- evaluate a resolved .dag declaration, or refuse -- proved unavailable by Y2-shaped EVIDENCE, the per-subject native roster. That does not follow. The roster is the denominator of a different replacement.\n\nWHAT THE BOOTSTRAP ACTUALLY LOOKS LIKE, which explains the frozen-X behavior far more directly than any roster: X currently interprets v2.compiler.eval, which MODELS the intended replacement semantics. That is X executing the SPECIFICATION of Y, not Y existing independently. Delete X today and the only general mechanism by which the v2 evaluator's own .dag implementation can execute goes with it.\n\nAND A THIRD EVALUATOR IS NOT THE ANSWER: authoring a fresh minimal interpreter beside these would make three authorities for evaluation behavior -- v1's semantics, v2.compiler.eval's semantics, and the new one's. The move is to make the evaluator that already exists independently real. A minimal EMIT-ABLE SLICE of v2.compiler.eval is a legitimate bootstrap increment, but only if it implements the existing interpretation algebra, shares the same runtime-value and diagnostic authorities, fails closed, never falls back to v1, and carries an explicit dissolution into the complete emitted evaluator -- that is a first increment of Y1, not a third semantic design.\n\nEMISSION IS THE FIRST BLOCKER AND NOT NECESSARILY THE LAST: v2.compiler.eval carries runtime_reject arms today. Even once it builds independently it may refuse real retained roots -- and THOSE REFUSALS ARE THE CENSUS THIS PROJECT HAS NEVER HAD, at the right grain: reachable evaluator forms, required primitive operations, required effects, required value and result representations. Not 744 declaration names and not 1350 Rust reference hits.\n\nA CONSEQUENCE WORTH CARRYING: on a boundary stated in consumer terms, cli_run is a CONSUMER of DagExecution and not automatically the parent cut. The unauthored-parent finding recorded in why_not_higher may therefore be an artifact of drawing the boundary at a FILE rather than at a contract, in which case it dissolves rather than needing to be solved. That arm is left as authored until it is re-measured at this boundary, because replacing a measured finding with a predicted one is the same error in the other direction.\n\nCENSUS GRAIN, declared: one row per distinct consuming module or binary, selected as the measured top of the reference distribution on main at 7f5fa6a94ec1 (1350 line-hits across 125 files; the ten rows below carry 1004 of them). This is NOT proven equal to the real boundary population -- the carrier states that limitation for every plan, and it holds here: an omitted consumer cannot be detected at this grain. The remaining 115 files are the residue this row does not enumerate, and that residue is a known omission rather than a claim of completeness.\n\nWHAT THAT REFERENCE COUNT DOES AND DOES NOT MEASURE: it measures ENTANGLEMENT -- how much code reaches X -- and it does not measure surviving OBLIGATIONS. Reliance establishes migration cost, never correctness, so the line-hit total is a selection instrument for census grain and nothing else. It must not be quoted as the size of the cut: the number that would be is how many of those references survive dispositioning, and that is not knowable until the boundary is settled.\n\nCORRECTION (row disposition, found by review after landing): coproduct_reflection and pre_push were authored Undecided on the stated ground that no disposition could be earned at an unauthored parent boundary. The measured branch DELETES both files, so this carrier reported an open decision for two consumers the artifact had already disposed of -- a false statement in a landed plan that other lanes read. Both are now Retire, because the deletion IS the disposition. cli_run remains Undecided and that is not the same defect: its region belongs to another lane, and parking a decision that is genuinely not this lane's to make is the honest arm rather than an error. Recorded here rather than silently amended, because a plan that corrects itself without saying so is indistinguishable from one that was always right.\n\nAND A DISPUTE THAT RIDES ON THOSE TWO ROWS, recorded because ratifying it silently would be the worse error: Retire here reports WHAT THE MEASURED BRANCH DID, not that deleting those consumers was methodologically sound. Review has since held that deleting consumers and behavioral sensors alongside X damages the experiment -- once a demand signal is removed, the absence of further failure no longer means the behavior was not load-bearing, it may only mean nothing is left to ask. A consumer earns Retire by being dispositioned, not by having been deleted. These two rows are therefore accurate as a record of the branch and are NOT a finding that the behavior had no surviving subject."

data interpreter_cut_identity: ReplacementCutId = "cut-v1-interpreter" as ReplacementCutId

fn nes(s: String) -> NonEmptyStr { s as NonEmptyStr }

fn consumer(name: String, disposition: ConsumerDisposition) -> LegacyConsumerSurveyRow {
  LegacyConsumerSurveyRow { consumer_label: nes(s: name), disposition: disposition }
}

// Retired: every row whose ONLY subject is X itself. These are not losses --
// their subject leaves with X, so retaining them would be retaining a claim
// about a thing that no longer exists.
fn retired_with_x() -> List<LegacyConsumerSurveyRow> {
  [
    consumer(
      name: "gunbc.v1_interpreter_primitive_surface",
      disposition: Retire { reason: nes(s: "its entire subject is X's primitive dispatch arms") },
    ),
    consumer(
      name: "v1_compiler.v1_interpreter_dispatch_generated",
      disposition: Retire { reason: nes(s: "generated projection of X's dispatch table; no authority survives it") },
    ),
    consumer(
      name: "gunbc.v1_interpreter_dispatch_emit",
      disposition: Retire { reason: nes(s: "the emitter whose only output is X's dispatch table") },
    ),
  ]
}

// Retired for a DIFFERENT reason, and kept in their own bucket because of it:
// these are not rows whose subject is X. They are consumers the measured branch
// deleted outright. Folding them in above would have let one bucket comment
// describe two classes -- a description that no longer matches its contents is
// the same stale-claim defect this carrier was just corrected for.
fn retired_by_deletion_on_the_measured_branch() -> List<LegacyConsumerSurveyRow> {
  [
    consumer(
      name: "v1_compiler.coproduct_reflection",
      disposition: Retire { reason: nes(s: "deleted by the measured cut; the deletion is the disposition, not a finding that the behavior had no surviving subject") },
    ),
    consumer(
      name: "v1_compiler.pre_push",
      disposition: Retire { reason: nes(s: "deleted by the measured cut; the deletion is the disposition, not a finding that the behavior had no surviving subject") },
    ),
  ]
}

// Undecided, and honestly so: the driver region belongs to another lane's
// boundary, and the cut above BOTH lanes has not been authored. A disposition
// here would be authored by the wrong lane at the wrong boundary.
//
// CORRECTED: this arm previously also carried coproduct_reflection and pre_push
// as Undecided. The measured branch DELETES both files, so the row reported an
// open decision for two consumers the artifact had already disposed of -- a
// false statement in a landed carrier that other lanes read. The deletion IS
// the disposition; both moved to Retire below. cli_run stays here because its
// region genuinely belongs to another lane and parking it is not an error.
fn undecided_at_the_parent_boundary() -> List<LegacyConsumerSurveyRow> {
  [
    consumer(name: "v1_compiler.cli_run", disposition: Undecided),
  ]
}

// Surviving with no coverage authority to name. Y does not cover them today and
// saying otherwise is the whole class this carrier exists to refuse.
fn surviving_without_coverage() -> List<LegacyConsumerSurveyRow> {
  [
    consumer(
      name: "v1_compiler.bin.claim_executor",
      disposition: RetainSameContract {
        justification: nes(s: "the floor executor must keep executing every enrolled witness"),
        coverage: CoverageOpen,
      },
    ),
    consumer(
      name: "v1_compiler.bin.interp_recorded_fixture_witness",
      disposition: RetainSameContract {
        justification: nes(s: "recorded-fixture replay has no native route"),
        coverage: CoverageOpen,
      },
    ),
    consumer(
      name: "v1_compiler.bin.auth_declared_but_unwired_witness",
      disposition: RetainSameContract {
        justification: nes(s: "probe binary still reached through X's evaluation entry"),
        coverage: CoverageOpen,
      },
    ),
  ]
}

// Already carried by an authority that is not X: the primitive identity join was
// re-grounded on its four surviving surfaces when X's roster left.
fn regrounded_on_y() -> List<LegacyConsumerSurveyRow> {
  [
    consumer(
      name: "std.primitive_identity",
      disposition: ReplacedByY { new_authority: nes(s: "std.primitive_identity four-surface derived census") },
    ),
  ]
}

fn interpreter_cut_consumers() -> List<LegacyConsumerSurveyRow> {
  concat(
    concat(retired_with_x(), retired_by_deletion_on_the_measured_branch()),
    concat(
      concat(undecided_at_the_parent_boundary(), surviving_without_coverage()),
      regrounded_on_y(),
    ),
  )
}

fn interpreter_cut_region() -> List<NonEmptyStr> {
  [
    nes(s: "v1_compiler.v1_interpreter"),
    nes(s: "v1_compiler.v1_interpreter_dispatch_generated"),
    nes(s: "gunbc.v1_interpreter_primitive_surface"),
    nes(s: "gunbc.v1_interpreter_dispatch_emit"),
  ]
}

// X's permitted external roles. The standing operator grant is compile-checking
// only: a main-built binary containing X may typecheck sources on a branch that
// has deleted X. It is never a fallback, never an internal resolver, never a
// production second opinion -- and none of those has a constructor here, which
// is what makes the grant structural rather than remembered.
fn interpreter_cut_external_x() -> ExternalXAnswer {
  ExternalXRoles { roles: [ExternalDifferentialOracle, HistoricalMeasurementSource] }
}

fn interpreter_cut_evidence() -> List<EvidenceDisposition> {
  [
    ReenrollAgainstY {
      evidence: nes(s: "cross_representation_equality guard witness"),
      surviving_claim: nes(s: "a cross-representation equality straddle refuses rather than answering false"),
    },
    RetireEvidence {
      evidence: nes(s: "interpreter_dispatch_bijection_real_roster_red"),
      retired_subject: nes(s: "bijection between X's dispatch arms and its declared roster"),
      retirement_reason: nes(s: "the bijection's two sides both leave with X") ,
    },
  ]
}

fn interpreter_cut_why_not_higher() -> HigherBoundaryAnswer {
  DescentParentUnauthored {
    intended_parent_boundary: nes(s: "the engine cut through the v1 driver region"),
    basis: nes(s: "no cut is authored at that boundary by any lane: the cli_run lane's ruled scope is the driver verbs and it places this region explicitly outside its own, so both lanes descend from one unauthored parent"),
  }
}

fn interpreter_replacement_survey() -> LegacyReplacementSurvey {
  LegacyReplacementSurvey {
    old_root_consumer: nes(s: "v1 .dag evaluation entry"),
    subject: nes(s: "every .dag subject executed by the v1 tree-walking interpreter: enrolled witnesses, plan evaluation, and lens execution"),
    contract: nes(s: "evaluate a resolved .dag declaration to a value, or refuse with a typed located diagnostic; no fabricated plausible output"),
    new_root: nes(s: "the DagExecution boundary: evaluate(resolved_graph, entry, arguments, execution_mode, capabilities) -> Value | LocatedDiagnostic | UnsupportedFeature"),
    why_not_higher: interpreter_cut_why_not_higher(),
    covered_region_labels: interpreter_cut_region(),
    consumer_rows: interpreter_cut_consumers(),
    external_x: interpreter_cut_external_x(),
    evidence_rows: interpreter_cut_evidence(),
    measured_revision: nes(s: "7f5fa6a94ec1"),
    limitation: nes(s: "GRAIN: ONE ROW PER CONSUMING MODULE OR BINARY, TEN OF 125 FILES. Not a complete consumer census: an omitted consumer is undetectable at this grain, and the 1350 line-hits behind the sample measure ENTANGLEMENT (how much code reaches X) rather than surviving OBLIGATIONS. Four of the ten labels -- v1_compiler.cli_run, v1_compiler.coproduct_reflection, v1_compiler.pre_push, v1_compiler.v1_interpreter_dispatch_generated -- name host Rust compilation units and not .dag modules, so at exact grain they are Rust ITEMS projected through gunbc.rust_item_host_observation item_declaration_ref, and one label may expand into many item relations or into none. v1_interpreter_dispatch_generated additionally appears BOTH as a consumer and as a covered-region member, which at exact grain is very likely a region member that was never an inbound boundary crossing at all."),
  }
}

// The registration, and the reason it is the remeasurement arm rather than an exact cut. The survey
// above was authored at module-or-binary grain against a ten-of-125 sample. The exact carrier's
// relation is consumer x consumed-authority at DECLARATION grain. Those are different identity
// universes, and translating the ten rows one-for-one would invent identities the measurement never
// established -- most visibly for the four host-Rust labels, where the exact consumers are Rust
// ITEMS and a single label may expand into many relations, into an uncitable impl method, or into
// nothing. Registering the obligation keeps the identity visible so a descent naming it resolves and
// refuses for the right reason, while making the coarse survey unable to answer as an exact plan.
fn interpreter_cut_registration() -> CutRegistration {
  RegisteredCutRemeasureRequired {
    identity: interpreter_cut_identity,
    prior_survey: interpreter_replacement_survey(),
    reason: nes(s: "authored at module-or-binary grain over a ten-of-125 sample; the exact carrier requires declaration-grain consumer x consumed-authority relations, and four of the ten labels name host Rust compilation units whose exact consumers are Rust items reached through gunbc.rust_item_host_observation. Remeasurement belongs to the lane that owns this boundary."),
  }
}
